<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_using-symmetry" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Using Symmetry</title>
	
<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 1-hour interactive classroom, after students have completed the rest of the chapter:
	</p>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>

<activity xml:id="class_infinite-slab">
		<title>Infinite Slab</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A charged wire with length <m>L</m> is oriented horizontally. The wire has a positive uniform charge density (the charge is spread out uniformly along the one-dimensional wire).
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="fig_slab">
		<title>Infinite Slab Figure</title>
    	<caption>Two views of a charged slab extending infinitely in the x- and y-directions.</caption>
    		<image source="Slab.png" width = "100%" >
				<description>Left, a 3D view of a rectangular slab that occupies the entire xy-plane, extending through a z-height of 2h.  Right, an edge view of the yz-plane, showing a rectangular cross section of the slab, extending infinitely in the horizontal y direction and vertically from -h to h in the z direction.  Point A lies above the slab on the left, point B lies above the slab on the right, point C lies in the slab on the right, and point D lies in the slab on the left, the four points forming a rectangle.</description>
			</image>
    </figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which direction should the electric field point?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How does the magnitude of the electric field at A and B compare? How does the magnitude of the electric field at C and D compare?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which method should you use: Gauss’s Law or chop-multiply-add? How do you know?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the following steps, first for <m>z \gt h</m> (outside), and then for <m>z \lt h</m> (inside).
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Draw an appropriate Gaussian surface.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine the enclosed charge.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Write down the flux through each surface.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Use Gauss's Law to determine the magnitude of the electric field. Do not forget your direction!
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</activity>

	<activity xml:id="class_finite-slab">
		<title>Finite Slab</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Suppose you have a finite slab of thickness <m>2h</m>, length <m>2a</m>, and width <m>2b</m>, with a uniform volume charge density <m>\rho</m>. You want to determine the electric field everywhere in space.
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="fig_slab2">
		<title>Finite Slab Figure</title>
    	<caption>Two views of a finite charged slab.</caption>
    		<image source="Slab2.png" width = "100%" >
				<description>Left, a 3D view of a rectangular slab that has x-length 2a, y-width 2b, and z-height 2h.  Right, an edge view of the yz-plane, showing a rectangular cross section of the slab, extending horizontally in the y direction from -b to b and vertically from -h to h in the z direction.  Point A lies above the middle of the slab on the z-axis and point B lies horizontally to the right from point A.</description>
			</image>
    </figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					True or False: The direction of the electric field at point A and B is the same.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					False.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Try setting up an integral using chop-multiply-add for no more than five minutes before you click on the hint.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<hint>
				<p>
					Setting up this integral is extremely challenging, and evaluating it analytically is nearly impossible.  Click the solution to see what it looks like!
				</p>
			</hint>
			<solution>
				<figure xml:id="fig_slab3">
		<title>Slab Calculation Figure</title>
    	<caption>Two views of an infinitesimal chunk of a finite charged slab.</caption>
    		<image source="Slab3.png" width = "70%" >
				<description>Top, a 3D view of a rectangular slab, with a small cube representing the infinitesimal piece.  Bottom, an edge view of the yz-plane, showing the small cube at position r prime and the position r where the field is to be evaluated.</description>
			</image>
    </figure>
				<me>
					\vec{E}(x,y,z) = k\rho\int_{-h}^h \int_{-b}^b \int_{-a}^a \frac{\left(x - x'\right)\hat{x} + \left(y - y'\right)\hat{y} + \left(z - z'\right)\hat{z}}{\left(\left(x - x'\right)^2 + \left(y - y'\right)^2 + \left(z - z'\right)^2\right)^{3/2}}dx'dy'dz'
				</me>
				
				<p>
					Calculating the electric field for any given charge distribution is in general non-trivial. This is why you try to apply any symmetry arguments before diving into the chop-multiply-add method.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</activity>

	


	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

